# awk: served by host busybox via the `/usr/bin/awk -> /usr/bin/busybox`
# symlink set up in build/docker/Dockerfile.debian. Required by
# autoconf's `AC_PROG_AWK` probe in rules_foreign_cc's pkgconfig
# bootstrap configure script; busybox awk is POSIX-conformant which is
# all AC_PROG_AWK needs.
build:hermetic-linux --sandbox_add_mount_pair=/usr/bin/busybox:/usr/bin/awk

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# host-<arch> + target-<arch> primitives, native + cross shortcuts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `host-<arch>`: exec-host-arch-dependent piece of the sandbox manifest.
# Today: just the dynamic loader bind-mount (see PT_INTERP note below).
#
# `target-<arch>`: target-arch-dependent build flags. Today: cc_toolchain
# registration via `--extra_toolchains` (chained via the `linux-<arch>`
# config in `build/.bazelrc`) and `--platforms=//platforms:linux_<arch>`
# so Bazel'\''s toolchain resolver targets the right arch. Without
# `--platforms`, target arch silently defaults to the host arch and
# `bazel-bin` resolves to `k8-fastbuild/` for arm64 builds (no
# cross-compile).
#
# Native shortcut (`hermetic-linux-<arch>` for host == target):
# compose the manifest + matching host + matching target. Activate
# with `--config=hermetic-linux-amd64` (or `-arm64`).
common:hermetic-linux-amd64 --config=hermetic-linux --config=host-amd64 --config=target-amd64
common:hermetic-linux-arm64 --config=hermetic-linux --config=host-arm64 --config=target-arm64

# Cross-compile shortcut (`hermetic-linux-cross-<target>`): builds for
# `<target>` from the other arch as exec host (the typical CI shape).
# `cross-arm64` runs on an amd64 host; `cross-amd64` runs on an arm64
# host. Power users who want a different host can compose
# `--config=host-<exec> --config=target-<tgt>` directly.
common:hermetic-linux-cross-arm64 --config=hermetic-linux --config=host-amd64 --config=target-arm64
common:hermetic-linux-cross-amd64 --config=hermetic-linux --config=host-arm64 --config=target-amd64

# target-<arch> primitive: cc_toolchain + platform for the target arch
common:target-amd64 --config=linux-amd64
common:target-amd64 --platforms=//platforms:linux_amd64
common:target-arm64 --config=linux-arm64
common:target-arm64 --platforms=//platforms:linux_arm64

# PT_INTERP of every dynamic ELF on Linux is the literal absolute path
# of the dynamic loader baked into the binary by the linker at link
# time (`/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2` on amd64,
# `/lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1` on arm64). The KERNEL reads PT_INTERP
# and `mmap`s that exact path BEFORE the binary starts;
# `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` and any other userspace env mechanism does NOT
# apply because resolution is pre-userspace. So whatever the literal
# PT_INTERP string says, the file must exist at that exact path
# inside the chroot.
#
# Empirical: the loader bind-mount is irreducible. Both the Debian-
# sourced `@llvm_toolchain_<arch>` clang / lld / etc. AND every binary
# clang+lld produces (PG, plperl, citus, RFCC bootstrap make, autoconf
# `conftest`) have the canonical PT_INTERP hardcoded; removing this
# bind-mount fails the toolchain itself with `cannot execute: required
# file not found`.
#
# This is the manifest's ONLY arch-specific entry. Every other runtime
# `.so` reaches its consumer through one of the project's own
# resolution mechanisms, all of them hermetic (do NOT depend on host
# files outside this manifest):
#
#   - `cc_wrapper.sh` (patched in
#     `build/patches/toolchains_llvm/0003-cc_wrapper-export-LD_LIBRARY_PATH-
#     from-toolchain-lib.patch`) exports `LD_LIBRARY_PATH=
#     ${toolchain_path_prefix}lib` so Debian clang's NEEDED libs
#     (libclang-cpp.so.14, libLLVM-14.so.1, libstdc++.so.6,
#     libgcc_s.so.1, libc.so.6, ...) resolve via the layout adapter's
#     `@llvm_toolchain_<arch>/lib/` for all compile and link actions
#     routed through the wrapper.
#
#   - Per-tool shims installed by
#     `//toolchains/llvm_sysroot:cc_toolchain_layout.bzl` cover the
#     `ar` / `nm` / `ld` / `objcopy` / `strip` / `dwp` / `cov` /
#     `profdata` / `objdump` `tool_paths` entries that bypass
#     `cc_wrapper.sh`. Same LD_LIBRARY_PATH, derived from `$0` + the
#     adapter's baked-in canonical repo name.
#
#   - `sysroot_setup.sh` populates `/lib/<cpu>-linux-gnu/` and
#     `/usr/lib/<cpu>-linux-gnu/` from the per-PG sysroot's multiarch
#     trees at action time for `pg_build` / `pgxs_build` actions.
#     PG-runtime ELFs (perl, msgfmt, llvm-config, ...) find their
#     NEEDED libs through ld.so's default search list.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KNOWN GAP and FUTURE PATH (loader-only manifest implications)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The loader-only manifest does not cover compiled output binaries
# that the build itself then EXECUTES inside the sandbox. The shim's
# `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` flows to the linker invocation but NOT to the
# produced ELF's own runtime env. Autoconf-style probes
# (`./conftest`, `./a.out`) and exec-config tool bootstraps
# (rules_foreign_cc's `make` and `pkgconfig` bootstrapping from
# source) WILL FAIL at runtime without libc.so.6 at the canonical
# multiarch path `/lib/<cpu>-linux-gnu/`. Concretely,
# `BootstrapGNUMake` exits with:
#
#   /external/+_repo_rules+llvm_toolchain_amd64/bin/clang.real:
#     cannot execute: required file not found
#
# OR:
#
#   configure: error: cannot run C compiled programs.
#
# depending on whether the test program itself or clang.real triggers
# the missing-libc failure first. `pg_build` / `pgxs_build` are not
# affected because `sysroot_setup.sh`'s symlink loop populates the
# multiarch paths from the per-PG sysroot at action time.
#
# Future-fix path: OPTION 1 - patchelf + cc_toolchain `--dynamic-linker`
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Replace this irreducible bind-mount with a fully hermetic loader by:
#
#   1. Add a `patchelf` repository_rule step (or extend
#      `//toolchains/llvm_sysroot:cc_toolchain_layout.bzl`) that
#      rewrites `PT_INTERP` on every adapter ELF (`bin/clang.real`,
#      `bin/lld.real`, `bin/ar.real`, ...) to an execroot-stable
#      sandbox-internal path like
#      `/execroot/_main/external/+_repo_rules+llvm_toolchain_<arch>/lib/ld-linux-<arch>.so.<N>`.
#      Bazel-resolved at adapter-build time via `rctx.name` (same
#      mechanism the per-tool shims use today for LD_LIBRARY_PATH).
#
#   2. Add `-Wl,--dynamic-linker=<same execroot-stable path>` to the
#      cc_toolchain's default LDFLAGS via a new `cc_toolchain_config`
#      feature. Every NEW binary the project links (PG, plperl,
#      conftest, RFCC bootstrap `make`, ...) gets the same hermetic
#      PT_INTERP baked in. The path is substituted from a label-
#      resolved attr at rule-impl time so no canonical bzlmod name
#      appears in human-authored source.
#
#   3. While at (2), inject `LD_LIBRARY_PATH=
#      <execroot-stable adapter lib>` for the same actions via an
#      `env_set` feature. This handles libc / libgcc_s / libstdc++ /
#      ... at runtime for binaries the build itself executes
#      (conftest, RFCC bootstrap). This is the plan's original
#      Commit 4, deferred from the mode-4 arc.
#
#   4. Verify: a build with this manifest emptied (zero bind-mount-
#      pairs for arch-specific paths) succeeds end-to-end through
#      `BootstrapGNUMake` + `BootstrapPkgConfig` + `pg_build` +
#      `pgxs_build` + `tar`.
#
# Tradeoffs to weigh during implementation:
#
#   - The final PG runtime binary (`bin/postgres`, `bin/initdb`,
#     `lib/plperl.so`, ...) would have a non-standard PT_INTERP and
#     cannot be `chroot`-less-run on a normal Linux host. The
#     packaging step would need a second `patchelf --set-interpreter`
#     pass to restore `PT_INTERP=/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2` (or the
#     downstream container would carry the hermetic loader at the
#     baked path). Mirror Phase A's distinction between build-time
#     ABI lockstep and runtime-image portability.
#
#   - Action sandbox path stability matters: Bazel's `linux-sandbox`
#     uses `/execroot/_main/...` as the action CWD, so PT_INTERP
#     pointing at `/execroot/_main/external/.../lib/ld-linux-...` is
#     valid inside every action. Outside the sandbox (e.g. `bazel
#     run`, or extracted tar files), the binary will not run; that
#     is acceptable for build-time artefacts but NOT for the final
#     runtime image.
#
#   - `patchelf` is a build-time tool; install via `build.nix` or
#     the project's docker base image. Pinned version matters
#     because patchelf has historically had compatibility quirks
#     with newer ELF features (DT_RELR, etc.); pin and document.
# `host-<arch>` is keyed by EXEC host arch (where actions execute), so
# cross-compile shortcuts (`hermetic-linux-cross-<target>`) compose
# `--config=host-<exec-arch>` and pick up the corresponding loader.
# Cross-compiled TARGET binaries never execute during the build (PG
# meson is cross-aware; exec-config tools run on the host), so the
# target arch's loader is unneeded when cross-compiling.
build:host-amd64 --sandbox_add_mount_pair=/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2
build:host-arm64 --sandbox_add_mount_pair=/lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1
